# php cannot lex a double-quoted string, and this is the whole of it.
#
# `encapsed_string_chars` is one of the twelve externals php declares and the
# coverage gate reports php seating NONE of them. So the body of `"x"` is a
# token no lexer in this tree can make, and joints says so itself:
#
#   unexpected (?:\\?[^'\\]+) at 12 in state 68
#   [no stand-in for encapsed_string_chars, admitted by shift]
#
# The claims below are TREE-SITTER'S answer over these same bytes, not
# joints's. They fail today - 5 roots, 1 mend, no `encapsed_string`, and a
# `text` node instead. That is the handover: this file passes when php can
# lex a string, and until then it says exactly which token is missing.
#
# Why it matters far past one string: `text` is php's node for inline HTML
# OUTSIDE `<?php`, so the recovery does not orphan the remainder - it CLAIMS
# it. See `text-swallows-remainder.php`, and in the corpus `Str.php`, where
# the first double-quoted string appears at byte 26,850 and the 40,995 bytes
# after it become one `text` node counted `built`.
roots 1
mends 0
holds encapsed_string
lacks text
spans encapsed_string 11 14
spans string_content 12 13
